  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM EDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nRain should lessen with time across the southernmost counties, with \nlight mist, sprinkles, or low clouds possible for much of the area \nuntil a significantly drier airmass pushes through overnight tonight \nfrom the north. This will conclude this beneficial bout of rain. \nPatchy fog tonight is possible before lower dewpoints arrive from \nthe north.\n\nFor the bulk of the work week we'll be in a benign regime, with the \nstronger polar jet to our north and an uninterested and weaker \nsubtropical jet to our south. Airmass across the region will remain \npleasantly warm, so daytime afternoon temperatures will remain near \n80F, or slight above that late week. Low temperatures will return \nback to the mid to low 50s with the disappearance of the low clouds. \nDry weather will dominate the week with no forcing mechanism for \nprecipitation and PWAT values beginning rather dry before moderating \ncloser to median percentiles. \n\nMost likely the next best chance of rain holds off until the \nweekend. Euro and GFS both depict a decently strong shortwave in the \nsubtropical jet arriving next weekend, with their ensemble means \nstarting to cluster in that direction. Interestingly the EC-AI \nensemble has not moved in this direction yet, so confidence can only \nbe described as low to medium at this time range. Still, this system \nwould bear watching as the models' orientation and strength of the \n850 wind field both would indicate another shot of healthy rainfall \nplus maybe the first wind event of the season.",
